@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@cindex introduction
@cindex EIGHTBOL compiler
@cindex eight front-ends

EIGHTBOL is a compiler for 8-bit and 16-bit microprocessors.  Programs
are written in one of eight source languages and compiled to assembly
source, which is then assembled by the platform's ordinary assembler
(such as 64tass, for the Atari 7800 and other 6502-family systems).

This manual is intended to be useful both to a first-time user who wants
to write and compile a small program, and to an experienced user who
needs the exact grammar of every statement a front end accepts.  Each
language chapter is organized in the style of the Commodore Programmer's
Reference Guides: it opens with the overall structure of a program,
enumerates every statement the front end understands with its exact
syntax, describes the operators and expressions, and closes with the
things the language commonly offers that EIGHTBOL deliberately does
@emph{not} support.  In particular, @strong{none of the eight front ends
performs file or console I/O}: there is no @code{PRINT}, @code{READ},
@code{INPUT}, @code{OPEN}, or @code{CLOSE}.  Programs interact with the
machine through global variables and through platform subroutines invoked
with the @code{INVOKE} or @code{CALL} mechanisms.

@menu
* About EIGHTBOL::              What EIGHTBOL is and is not
* Installing EIGHTBOL::         Dependencies and setup
* Invoking EIGHTBOL::           Command-line usage and file selection
* Program Structure::           Programs, classes, methods, and routines
* Data and Parameters::         Variables, storage, and register arguments
* A Guided Tour::               A small annotated example program
@end menu

@node About EIGHTBOL
@section About EIGHTBOL

@cindex COBOL heritage
@cindex eight languages

EIGHTBOL takes its name and its original grammar from COBOL (COmmon
Business Oriented Language), a language created in 1959 to help
businesses process data and generate reports.  Unlike many programming
languages that use cryptic symbols, COBOL was designed to read like
English sentences:

@example
ADD 1 TO Counter.
@end example

Reads almost like a sentence.  EIGHTBOL preserves that quality in the
COBOL front end and extends the same compiling machinery to seven more
languages: BASIC, FORTRAN, Pascal, Lua, Lingo, Objective-C, and
SmallTalk.  Every front end compiles into the same internal
representation, an abstract syntax tree, so the code generator and the
per-processor back ends are shared regardless of which language the
program was written in.

The languages are not all equally complete.  EIGHTBOL was developed with
COBOL as the flagship language, and the COBOL front end is the most
thoroughly implemented.  The BASIC front end implements a substantial
dialect.  The Pascal front end is functional but narrow.  The FORTRAN,
Lua, Lingo, Objective-C, and SmallTalk front ends are best regarded as
@emph{designed grammars}: they describe the syntax the languages intend
to accept, but the front ends are not yet wired into the command line,
and in places the grammars are incomplete.  Each language chapter says
plainly what is implemented, what is designed-but-not-implemented, and
what is not supported at all.

@node Installing EIGHTBOL
@section Installing EIGHTBOL

@cindex installation
@cindex Common Lisp
@cindex ASDF
@cindex Classes.Defs

EIGHTBOL is written in Common Lisp and is loaded with ASDF.  Its source
tree is a submodule of the Phantasia repository and lives at
@file{SkylineTool/eightbol/}.  To load it:

@example
(cd SkylineTool/eightbol)
sbcl --non-interactive --load go.lisp
@end example

or, from a Slime or SLIME-compatible REPL:

@example
(asdf:load-system :eightbol)
@end example

The test suite is run with:

@example
(asdf:test-system :eightbol)
@end example

@subsection The Classes.Defs File

@cindex Classes.Defs
@cindex class definitions
@cindex METHOD-ID

Before compiling a program that defines or uses classes, EIGHTBOL must
know about the classes.  Class and method declarations are stored in a
file named @file{Classes.Defs} (uppercase, with a hyphen replacing the
space).  The file is processed by a small tool that extracts the
available classes and methods and writes the tables EIGHTBOL uses for
name resolution and for generating class and method labels.

@table @asis
@item @file{Classes.Defs}
The master list of classes, written one class per line in the form
@code{#Class/@var{Class-Name}}, optionally followed by method names in
the form @code{#/@var{Method-Name}}.
@item @file{Classes/Phantasia-Globals.cpy}
A generated COBOL copybook containing the global symbol table.
@end table

The Phantasia build regenerates these files automatically; a standalone
EIGHTBOL installation must provide them before compiling COBOL or BASIC
programs that reference classes.

@node Invoking EIGHTBOL
@section Invoking EIGHTBOL

@cindex command line
@cindex @code{--basic}
@cindex .cob files
@cindex .bas files

The compiled driver is the @command{eightbol} executable (built by the
Phantasia Makefile as @file{SkylineTool/bin/eightbol}).  The command
line has the form:

@example
eightbol [--basic] @var{source-file} ...
@end example

The front end is chosen from the file name suffix:

@table @asis
@item @code{.cob}
COBOL source; compiled with the COBOL front end.  This is the default
when no option is given.
@item @code{--basic @var{file.bas}}
BASIC source; compiled with the BASIC front end.  The @code{--basic}
option must precede the file name.
@end table

At the time of writing, only the COBOL and BASIC front ends are
reachable from the command line.  The Pascal, FORTRAN, Lua, Lingo,
Objective-C, and SmallTalk front ends are not yet wired into the driver;
their chapters document the grammar they intend to implement.

@node Program Structure
@section Program Structure

@cindex standalone program
@cindex object method
@cindex routine

Every source file compiles to a single object: either a standalone
@emph{program} or routine, or an object @emph{method}.  The two shapes
are nearly the same in all eight languages; the difference is only in
the wrapper the front end expects.

@table @asis
@item Standalone program
A complete, compilable unit.  In COBOL this is a
@code{PROGRAM-ID}@dots{}@code{END PROGRAM} body; in BASIC a
@code{METHOD}@dots{}@code{END METHOD} or subroutine body; in the other
languages their corresponding top-level form.
@item Object method
A method named in @file{Classes.Defs}.  The method belongs to a class,
and the compiler arranges for the method's label to be the class and
method name so the object dispatcher can reach it.
@end table

The generated assembly defines the object's entry point with a label of
the form @code{Method@var{ClassName}@var{MethodName}} for the 6502
family (matching the OOPS convention), or the platform's equivalent for
other processors.

@node Data and Parameters
@section Data and Parameters

@cindex parameter passing
@cindex registers
@cindex global variables
@cindex storage

Memory allocation is a serious matter on 8-bit machines.  EIGHTBOL
therefore keeps parameter passing extremely simple:

@quotation
@strong{Parameter passing is limited to one register in and one register
out.}  Data is generally passed through (global) variables rather than
through an argument list.
@end quotation

Consequences of this rule:

@itemize
@item A method or routine accepts at most a single argument, carried in
the machine's first argument register (@code{A} on the 6502 family,
@code{A} on the Z80, and so on).
@item It returns at most a single value, in the same register.
@item Anything larger --- strings, records, arrays --- is communicated by
writing it to a global variable before the call and reading that
variable after the call.
@item The compilers allocate storage from a fixed address table; the
assembler source names storage with labels derived from the variable
names.
@end itemize

Each language chapter repeats this rule where it matters, because it is
the single most important difference between EIGHTBOL and a desktop
compiler for the same language.

@node A Guided Tour
@section A Guided Tour

@cindex example
@cindex guided tour

To give the shape of the manual, here is a complete small COBOL program
that sets a global flag and returns:

@example
       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
       PROGRAM-ID. SetFlag.
       DATA DIVISION.
       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
       01 Result PIC 9 VALUE 0.
       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
       Main.
           MOVE 1 TO Result
           GOBACK.
       END PROGRAM SetFlag.
@end example

The same effect in BASIC:

@example
METHOD SetFlag
  Result = 1
END METHOD
@end example

Both compile to assembly that stores 1 in the @code{Result} variable and
returns.  The chapter on each language explains the syntax in detail and
gives more complete examples, including object methods and loops.

@node BASIC
@chapter BASIC

@cindex BASIC
@cindex Dartmouth BASIC
@cindex Microsoft BASIC

The BASIC front end compiles programs written in a dialect descended from
Dartmouth BASIC, with a number of structured-programming extensions
borrowed from later dialects and from EIGHTBOL's own conventions.  A
program is a sequence of statements separated by colons (@code{:}).  A
statement may carry an optional line number and an optional string label.

Each front-end chapter is arranged as follows: structure of a program,
statement-by-statement reference, operators and expressions, storage, and
finally the constructs the language normally offers that EIGHTBOL does
not implement.

@menu
* BASIC Program Structure::     Lines, colons, classes, methods
* BASIC Statements::            Reference to every BASIC statement
* BASIC Operators and Expressions::  Arithmetic, relational, and logical
* BASIC Data::                  Variables and storage
* BASIC Standalone Programs::   Writing a routine or program
* BASIC Object Methods::        Writing a method on a class
* BASIC Unsupported Constructs::  What EIGHTBOL BASIC does not do
@end menu

@node BASIC Program Structure
@section Program Structure

@cindex line number
@cindex label
@cindex colon

A BASIC program is a sequence of @emph{statements}.  Statements are
separated by a colon (@code{:}) or by a newline.  Each statement may be
prefixed by an optional line number and an optional string label:

@example
100        REM a numbered line
MainLabel: WHILE Alive
           MOVE 1 TO Ticks
           WEND
           GOTO "MainLabel"
@end example

Line numbers are compiled into labels of the form
@code{BASIC_L@var{nnnnnn}}, so that @code{GOTO 100} jumps to the
assembly label @code{BASIC_L100}.  String labels are compiled verbatim
and may be used as the targets of @code{GOTO} and @code{GOSUB}.

A file may contain a main block followed by any number of @code{METHOD},
@code{PROCEDURE}, and @code{CLASS} declarations.  The main block is the
standalone program; the methods and procedures become separate callable
objects.  See @ref{BASIC Standalone Programs} and @ref{BASIC Object
Methods}.

@node BASIC Statements
@section Statements

@cindex LET
@cindex IF@dots{}THEN
@cindex FOR@dots{}TO@dots{}STEP
@cindex NEXT
@cindex WHILE
@cindex UNTIL
@cindex DO@dots{}NEXT
@cindex LOG FAULT
@cindex STOP
@cindex GOTO
@cindex GOSUB
@cindex RETURN
@cindex METHOD
@cindex PROCEDURE
@cindex CLASS
@cindex END
@cindex INVOKE
@cindex CALL
@cindex MOVE
@cindex SET
@cindex ASSEMBLY ENTRY
@cindex COPY

This section lists every statement the BASIC front end accepts, with its
syntax and a short description.  Metavariables are written in
lowercase; keywords are written uppercase.

@table @asis
@item @code{@var{variable} = @var{expression}}
Assignment (same as @code{LET}); compiles to a @code{:move} AST node.
@item @code{LET @var{variable} = @var{expression}}
Explicit assignment; also a @code{:move}.
@item @code{IF @var{condition} THEN @var{statement}}
Conditional execution; no @code{ELSE} required.
@item @code{IF @var{condition} THEN @var{statement} ELSE @var{statement}}
Conditional execution with alternative.
@item @code{FOR @var{variable} = @var{start} TO @var{end}}
Counted loop; equivalent to @code{PERFORM FOR-@var{variable} VARYING}
compiled as a @code{:perform} node.
@item @code{FOR @var{variable} = @var{start} TO @var{end} STEP @var{step}}
Counted loop with explicit increment.
@item @code{NEXT}
End of a @code{FOR} loop.  The optional variable of classic BASIC is
accepted but ignored.
@item @code{WHILE @var{condition} @var{statement}}
Loop while the condition holds; compiled as a conditional.
@item @code{UNTIL @var{condition} @var{statement}}
Loop until the condition holds.
@item @code{DO : @var{statement-list} NEXT WHILE @var{condition}}
Repeat a statement list while a condition holds.  Statements within the
list are separated by colons.
@item @code{DO : @var{statement-list} NEXT UNTIL @var{condition}}
Repeat a statement list until a condition holds.
@item @code{LOG FAULT "@var{code}"}
Signal the fault handler with the given fault code.
@item @code{STOP "@var{code}"}
Halt execution with the given stop code.
@item @code{GOTO @var{target}}
Unconditional jump.  @var{target} is a line number (compiled to
@code{BASIC_L@var{nnnnnn}}), a string label, or an identifier.
@item @code{GOSUB @var{target}}
Call a subroutine and return with @code{RETURN}.  Four variants:
@table @asis
@item @code{GOSUB "@var{label}"}
Local subroutine call.
@item @code{GOSUB @var{ident}}
Subroutine call by identifier.
@item @code{GOSUB "@var{name}" IN LIBRARY}
Resident-library call; assembles to @code{jsr Lib.@var{Name}} on the 6502.
@item @code{GOSUB "@var{method}" ON @var{object}}
OOPS method invocation (same as @code{INVOKE}).
@item @code{GOSUB "@var{name}" SERVICE}
Service-dispatch call; the bank is resolved at link time.
@end table
@item @code{RETURN}
Return from a @code{GOSUB}; compiles to a @code{:goback}.
@item @code{METHOD "@var{Name}" ON "@var{Class}"}
Begin an object method named @var{Name} on class @var{Class}.  The rest
of the file until @code{END} is the method body.
@item @code{PROCEDURE "@var{Name}"}
Begin a named standalone routine.
@item @code{CLASS "@var{Name}"}
Set the current class for subsequent methods.
@item @code{END}
End the current method, procedure, or program; compiles to a
@code{:goback}.
@item @code{INVOKE @var{object} "@var{Method}"}
Invoke method @var{Method} on @var{object}.
@item @code{CALL @var{target}}
Call a named target.
@item @code{MOVE @var{expression} TO @var{variable}}
Copy a value.
@item @code{SET @var{variable} TO @var{expression}}
Assign a value.
@item @code{ASSEMBLY ENTRY "@var{label}"}
Declare the assembly label at which this program or method is entered.
@item @code{COPY "@var{name}"}
Expand a copybook (such as a generated classes file).
@end table

@node BASIC Operators and Expressions
@section Operators and Expressions

@cindex operators
@cindex expressions
@cindex precedence

Expressions are built from literals, identifiers, @code{SELF}, and
@code{NULL}, combined with the usual operators.  Precedence is from
highest to lowest:

@table @asis
@item parentheses
@code{( @var{expression} )}
@item unary
@code{NOT}
@item multiplicative
@code{*} @code{/}
@item additive
@code{+} @code{-}
@item relational
@code{=} @code{<>} @code{<} @code{<=} @code{>} @code{>=}
@item logical
@code{AND}, then @code{OR}
@end table

The relational and logical operators produce @code{:conditional-*},
@code{:and}, @code{:or}, and @code{:not} AST nodes.  The arithmetic
operators produce @code{:expression-add}, @code{:expression-subtract},
@code{:expression-multiply}, and @code{:expression-divide} nodes.

@cindex SELF
@cindex NULL
@code{SELF} refers to the current object and @code{NULL} to a null
reference; both may appear where an expression is expected.

@node BASIC Data
@section Data

@cindex variables
@cindex globals
@cindex storage

Variables are global.  Storage is allocated by the back end from the
fixed address tables, and the assembler refers to each variable by the
label derived from its name.  There are no dimensioned arrays and no
records in the current BASIC dialect; see @ref{BASIC Unsupported
Constructs}.  As with every front end, parameter passing is limited to a
single register in and a single register out; pass data through global
variables.  See @ref{Data and Parameters}.

@node BASIC Standalone Programs
@section Writing a Standalone Program

@cindex standalone program

A standalone BASIC program is a sequence of statements at the top level
of the file.  It is compiled as the @code{main-block} of the object.
Line numbers give the assembler a jump target for classic
@code{GOTO}/@code{GOSUB} flow.  Example --- a routine that counts down
from 10 to 1 in a global variable:

@example
METHOD "CountDown" ON "Demo"
  X = 10
  FOR I = 1 TO 10
    X = X - 1
  NEXT
END
@end example

A procedure, which is not tied to a class, is written with
@code{PROCEDURE}:

@example
PROCEDURE "Delay"
  Ticks = 200
  WHILE Ticks > 0
    Ticks = Ticks - 1
  WEND
END
@end example

@node BASIC Object Methods
@section Writing an Object Method

@cindex object method

An object method is introduced with @code{METHOD "@var{Name}" ON
"@var{Class}"} and terminated with @code{END}.  The method's assembly
entry label follows the OOPS convention (@code{Method@var{ClassName}@var{MethodName}}
on the 6502 family) so the object dispatcher can find it:

@example
METHOD "Heal" ON "Hero"
  HP = HP + 10
END
@end example

The class must be declared in @file{Classes.Defs} before compilation.
See @ref{Installing EIGHTBOL}.  Methods receive and return a single
register; anything more must travel through globals.

@node BASIC Unsupported Constructs
@section Constructs EIGHTBOL BASIC Does Not Support

@cindex unsupported
@cindex PRINT
@cindex INPUT
@cindex READ
@cindex OPEN
@cindex CLOSE
@cindex DIM
@cindex arrays
@cindex DATA
@cindex REM
@cindex DEF FN

The following constructs are common in BASIC dialects but are
@strong{not} recognized by the EIGHTBOL BASIC front end.  Do not use
them; the compiler will signal a syntax error (or, in the case of
unknown tokens, a token error).

@itemize
@item @code{PRINT} --- no console or screen output.
@item @code{INPUT} --- no terminal input.
@item @code{READ} --- no data-file input (there is no @code{DATA} either).
@item @code{OPEN} / @code{CLOSE} --- no file system.
@item @code{DIM} / array subscripting --- no dimensioned storage.
@item @code{DEF FN} --- no user-defined functions.
@item @code{REM} --- comments are not part of the grammar; use the
assembler's comment conventions or the platform's comment character.
@item Classic line-number-only flow with line-number arithmetic.
@end itemize

Interaction with the machine happens through global variables and
through @code{INVOKE}/@code{CALL}/@code{GOSUB@dots{}@var{kind}@dots{}}
calls to platform routines, never through I/O statements.

@node COBOL
@chapter COBOL

@cindex COBOL
@cindex EIGHTBOL heritage language

COBOL is EIGHTBOL's original and flagship language, and its front end is
the most complete.  The dialect is a business-oriented language whose
statements read like English sentences:

@example
ADD 1 TO Counter.
MOVE Name TO DisplayBuffer.
@end example

Every statement ends with a period.  Data is described separately from
code in a DATA DIVISION using level numbers and picture clauses, in the
standard COBOL manner.

@menu
* COBOL Program Structure::     Divisions, paragraphs, classes
* COBOL Data Division::         Level numbers, PIC, copybooks
* COBOL Statements::            Reference to every COBOL statement
* COBOL Conditions and Expressions::  Conditional syntax
* COBOL Object Methods::        Writing a method on a class
* COBOL Unsupported Constructs::  What EIGHTBOL COBOL does not do
@end menu

@node COBOL Program Structure
@section Program Structure

@cindex divisions
@cindex IDENTIFICATION DIVISION
@cindex DATA DIVISION
@cindex PROCEDURE DIVISION
@cindex PROGRAM-ID
@cindex paragraphs

A COBOL program is divided into divisions, of which three are used:

@itemize
@item @code{IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.} --- names the program with
@code{PROGRAM-ID. @var{name}.}
@item @code{DATA DIVISION.} --- declares storage (see @ref{COBOL Data
Division}).
@item @code{PROCEDURE DIVISION.} --- the executable statements.
@end itemize

The PROCEDURE DIVISION is organized into @emph{paragraphs}, each headed
by a paragraph name followed by a period.  Paragraphs are called with
@code{PERFORM}, exactly as in standard COBOL:

@example
       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
       PROGRAM-ID. Demo.
       DATA DIVISION.
       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
       01 Counter PIC 99 VALUE 0.
       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
       Main.
           PERFORM Bump
           GOBACK.
       Bump.
           ADD 1 TO Counter.
@end example

@node COBOL Data Division
@section Data Division

@cindex WORKING-STORAGE
@cindex level numbers
@cindex PIC
@cindex PICTURE
@cindex USAGE
@cindex VALUE
@cindex 01
@cindex copybook
@cindex COPY

Storage is declared in the @code{DATA DIVISION}, most often in the
@code{WORKING-STORAGE SECTION}.  Each data item begins on a line with a
level number, conventionally @code{01} for a top-level item and higher
numbers for subordinate items.  The picture clause @code{PIC} describes
the storage format:

@example
       01 Score PIC 99 VALUE 0.
       01 Name   PIC X(10).
       01 Amount PIC S99V9 COMP-3.
@end example

@table @asis
@item @code{PIC 9...} / @code{PIC X...}
Numeric / character storage.  Repetition counts such as @code{PIC X(10)}
are accepted.
@item @code{PIC S...}
Signed decimal; the sign counts as one nibble of storage.
@item @code{PIC V...}
Implied decimal point (no storage consumed).
@item @code{COMP-3} / @code{PACKED-DECIMAL} / @code{USAGE}
Storage usage qualifiers.
@item @code{VALUE @var{literal}}
Initial value.
@item @code{OCCURS @var{n}}
Repeated items.
@item @code{REDEFINES}
Overlapping storage.
@end table

Copybooks are expanded with @code{COPY @var{name}.} in the data
division.  EIGHTBOL's copybook reader accepts a token following
@code{COPY} and expands it from the generated copybook files such as
@file{Classes/Phantasia-Globals.cpy}.  See
@code{read-copybook} in @file{src/cobol-copybook.lisp}.

@node COBOL Statements
@section Statements

@cindex MOVE
@cindex ADD
@cindex SUBTRACT
@cindex COMPUTE
@cindex IF@dots{}ELSE
@cindex PERFORM
@cindex INVOKE
@cindex CALL
@cindex GOBACK
@cindex SET
@cindex STRING
@cindex INSPECT
@cindex EVALUATE
@cindex SEARCH
@cindex subscript

Every statement the COBOL front end accepts is listed below.  Statements
end with a period.

@table @asis
@item @code{MOVE @var{expression} TO @var{data-item}}
Copy a value.
@item @code{ADD @var{expression} TO @var{item}}
@itemx @code{ADD @var{expression} TO @var{item} GIVING @var{item}}
Arithmetic addition.
@item @code{SUBTRACT @var{expression} FROM @var{item}}
@itemx @code{SUBTRACT @var{expression} FROM @var{item} GIVING @var{item}}
Arithmetic subtraction.
@item @code{COMPUTE @var{item} = @var{expression}}
Arithmetic from an expression.
@item @code{IF @var{condition} @var{statement} [ELSE @var{statement}]}
Conditional execution.  @code{END-IF} is accepted.
@item @code{PERFORM @var{paragraph}}
Call a paragraph.
@item @code{PERFORM @var{paragraph} UNTIL @var{condition}}
Call a paragraph until a condition holds.
@item @code{PERFORM @var{paragraph} VARYING @var{item} FROM @var{expr} BY @var{expr} UNTIL @var{condition}}
Indexed loop.
@item @code{PERFORM @var{n} TIMES}
Fixed-iteration loop.
@item @code{INVOKE @var{object} "@var{Method}"}
Invoke a method on an object.  On the 6502 family this assembles to
@code{.CallMethod Call@var{Class}@var{Method}, @var{Class}Class}; other
processors keep their own dispatch.
@item @code{CALL @var{target}}
@itemx @code{CALL "@var{name}" IN LIBRARY}
@itemx @code{CALL SERVICE @var{name}}
Call a target: a plain identifier, a resident library routine
(@code{jsr Lib.@var{name}} on the 6502), or a service whose bank is
resolved at link time.
@item @code{GOBACK}
Return from the current program or paragraph.
@item @code{EXIT METHOD}
Return from a method.
@item @code{EXIT PROGRAM}
Return from a program.
@item @code{EXIT}
Terminate a paragraph.
@item @code{SET @var{item} TO @var{expression}}
Assign a value.  Other SET variants are unsupported (see @ref{COBOL
Unsupported Constructs}).
@item @code{STRING @var{source} DELIMITED BY SIZE INTO @var{destination}}
Block transfer (BLT) of a string of fixed size.
@item @code{INSPECT @var{item} TALLYING @var{counter} FOR @var{characters}}
@itemx @code{INSPECT @var{item} CONVERTING @var{a} TO @var{b}}
@itemx @code{INSPECT @var{item} REPLACING CHARACTERS BY @var{char}}
String inspection and translation.
@item @code{EVALUATE @var{expression} WHEN @var{value} @var{statement}}
@itemx @code{EVALUATE TRUE WHEN @var{condition} @var{statement}}
Multi-way branch.  @code{END-EVALUATE} is accepted.
@item @code{SEARCH @var{table} VARYING @var{item} WHEN @var{condition}}
Table search.  @code{END-SEARCH} is accepted.
@item @code{LOG FAULT @var{code}}
Signal a fault to the fault handler.
@item @code{BREAK}
Debug break; on the 6502 this fires the monitor's @code{brk}.
@item @code{DEBUG}
Debug statement.
@item @code{ASSEMBLY ENTRY "@var{label}"}
Declare the assembly entry label.
@item @code{COPY "@var{name}"}
Expand a copybook.
@item @code{CONTINUE}
No-op.
@end table

Subscripted access is supported for @code{OCCURS} items, e.g.
@code{MOVE List(Index) TO Item.} with the subscript in parentheses.

@node COBOL Conditions and Expressions
@section Conditions and Expressions

@cindex conditions
@cindex relational operators

Conditions compare expressions with the relational operators
@code{=}, @code{<}, @code{<=}, @code{>}, @code{>=}, and @code{<>}, plus
the spelled-out forms:

@example
@var{expr} IS GREATER THAN @var{expr}
@var{expr} IS LESS THAN OR EQUAL TO @var{expr}
@var{expr} IS EQUAL TO @var{expr}
@end example

Conditions may be combined with @code{AND} and @code{OR} and negated
with @code{NOT}.  Logical operators on bit values are available as
@code{AND}, @code{OR}, @code{XOR}, @code{NOT}, @code{BIT-AND},
@code{BIT-OR}, @code{BIT-XOR}, @code{BIT-NOT}, @code{SHIFT-LEFT}, and
@code{SHIFT-RIGHT}.  Literal constants @code{ZERO}, @code{HIGH-VALUES},
@code{LOW-VALUES}, @code{NULLS}, @code{SPACES}, and similar are
recognized.

@node COBOL Object Methods
@section Writing a Method on a Class

@cindex METHOD-ID

An object method is written as an @code{OBJECT} with a
@code{PROCEDURE DIVISION} (an optional inline @code{DATA DIVISION} is
accepted).  The method is named with @code{METHOD-ID. @var{Name}.}:

@example
       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
       PROGRAM-ID. Hero.
       OBJECT.
       DATA DIVISION.
       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
       01 HP PIC 999 VALUE 100.
       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
       METHOD-ID. Heal.
           ADD 10 TO HP.
           EXIT METHOD.
       END METHOD Heal.
       END OBJECT.
@end example

The class and method must be declared in @file{Classes.Defs}.  The
compiler generates the OOPS-compatible label @code{MethodHeroHeal} for
the 6502 family, regardless of hyphens in the @code{METHOD-ID} (a
hyphenated name such as @code{Song--Heal--ID} emits @code{Song_Heal_ID}).
See @ref{Data and Parameters} for the single-register calling rule.

@node COBOL Unsupported Constructs
@section Constructs EIGHTBOL COBOL Does Not Support

@cindex DIVIDE
@cindex MULTIPLY
@cindex UNSTRING
@cindex GOTO
@cindex ACCEPT
@cindex DISPLAY
@cindex READ
@cindex WRITE
@cindex OPEN
@cindex CLOSE

EIGHTBOL deliberately does not implement these COBOL constructs.  The
front end recognizes them and signals an @code{EIGHTBOL-SOURCE-ERROR}
with the message shown, rather than failing obscurely.

@table @asis
@item @code{ACCEPT}
Signals @code{ACCEPT is not supported}.  No terminal input.
@item @code{DISPLAY}
Signals @code{DISPLAY is not supported}.  No console output.
@item @code{READ}
Signals @code{READ is not supported}.  No file input.
@item @code{WRITE}
Signals @code{WRITE is not supported}.  No file output.
@item @code{OPEN}
Signals @code{OPEN is not supported}.  No file system.
@item @code{CLOSE}
Signals @code{CLOSE is not supported}.  No file system.
@item @code{DIVIDE}
@code{DIVIDE ... GIVING ...} is supported; @code{DIVIDE ... REMAINDER ...}
signals @code{DIVIDE ... REMAINDER ... is not supported}.
@item @code{MULTIPLY}
Not supported.
@item @code{UNSTRING}
Signals @code{UNSTRING is not supported}.
@item @code{GOTO}
Not supported; use @code{PERFORM} or @code{EVALUATE}.
@item @code{STRING @var{source} DELIMITED BY @var{character}}
Only @code{DELIMITED BY SIZE} is supported.  A character delimiter
signals @code{STRING with character delimiter is not supported; use
DELIMITED BY SIZE for BLT}.
@item @code{SET ... UP BY} / @code{SET ... DOWN BY}
Not supported.
@item @code{SET @var{condition-name} TO TRUE}
Signals @code{SET condition-name TO TRUE is not supported}.
@item @code{SET ... TO NULLS}
Signals @code{SET ... TO NULLS is not supported}.
@end table

@node FORTRAN
@chapter FORTRAN

@cindex FORTRAN
@cindex designed grammar

The FORTRAN front end compiles a dialect of FORTRAN that includes
structured-programming and object-oriented extensions borrowed from
EIGHTBOL's other front ends.  At the time of writing this front end is
@strong{not wired into the command line} and is best regarded as a
designed grammar: the compiler machinery exists, but the front end is not
yet active in the build, and the lexer has known limitations (see
@ref{FORTRAN Limitations}).  The grammar below is the grammar the front
end intends to accept.

@menu
* FORTRAN Program Structure::   Programs, subroutines, functions
* FORTRAN Statements::          Statement reference
* FORTRAN Expressions::         Arithmetic, relational, and logical
* FORTRAN Object Orientation::  OOPS classes and methods
* FORTRAN Limitations::         Designed grammar caveats
@end menu

@node FORTRAN Program Structure
@section Program Structure

@cindex PROGRAM
@cindex SUBROUTINE
@cindex FUNCTION

A FORTRAN program is introduced by @code{PROGRAM @var{name}}, followed by
a sequence of statements, and closed by @code{END @var{name}}:

@example
PROGRAM CountDown
  INTEGER I
  I = 10
  DO 100 I = 1, 10
    I = I - 1
  ENDDO
  STOP
END CountDown
@end example

Subroutines and functions use the conventional @code{SUBROUTINE} and
@code{FUNCTION} forms, each compiled as a method node:

@example
SUBROUTINE Bump
  I = I + 1
  RETURN
END Bump

FUNCTION Double(X) INTEGER
  Double = X * 2
  RETURN
END Double
@end example

Note that the subroutines and functions compile to @code{:method} AST
nodes, and @code{FUNCTION} return values follow the language's assignment
convention (assign to the function name).

@node FORTRAN Statements
@section Statements

@table @asis
@item @code{PROGRAM @var{name} @dots{} END @var{name}}
Top-level program.
@item @code{SUBROUTINE @var{name}(@var{args}) @dots{} END @var{name}}
Subroutine definition; @var{args} are accepted but discarded.
@item @code{FUNCTION @var{name}(@var{args}) @var{type} @dots{} END @var{name}}
Function definition; the return type is accepted but discarded.
@item @code{@var{variable} = @var{expression}}
Assignment; compiles to a @code{:move}.
@item @code{CALL @var{name}(@var{args})}
Calls a named target.
@item @code{GOTO @var{label}}
Unconditional jump.
@item @code{RETURN}
Return from a subroutine or function; compiles to a @code{:goback}.
@item @code{STOP}
Halt; compiles to a @code{:stop-run}.
@item @code{IF (@var{condition}) THEN @dots{} ENDIF}
@itemx @code{IF (@var{condition}) THEN @dots{} ELSE @dots{} ENDIF}
Conditional execution.
@item @code{DO @var{variable} = @var{from}, @var{to} @dots{} ENDDO}
Counted loop; compiles to a @code{:perform} node.
@item @code{READ @var{unit}}
Recognized but compiles to a degenerate @code{:move} of the unit number;
there is no real input.
@item @code{WRITE @var{unit} @var{format}}
Recognized but compiles to a degenerate @code{:move}; there is no real
output.
@item @code{DATA @var{name} = @var{value}}
Compiles to a @code{:set}.
@item @code{PARAMETER @var{name} = @var{value}}
Compiles to a @code{:set}.
@item @code{INTEGER}, @code{REAL}, @code{LOGICAL}, @code{CHARACTER},
@code{COMPLEX} @var{name}
Type declarations; recorded in the type table, no code generated.
@item @code{COMMON @var{name}}
Recorded in the compiler tables, no code generated.
@item @code{DIMENSION @var{name}(@var{dims})}
Recorded in the compiler tables, no code generated.
@item @code{INCLUDE "@var{file}"}
Compiles to a @code{:copy} node.
@end table

@node FORTRAN Expressions
@section Expressions

@cindex exponentiation

Arithmetic expressions use @code{+}, @code{-}, @code{*}, @code{/}, and
exponentiation @code{**}.  The relational operators are @code{=},
@code{<>}, @code{<}, @code{<=}, @code{>}, and @code{>=}; logical
operators are @code{AND}, @code{OR}, and @code{NOT}.  Literals are
numbers and strings; @code{TRUE} and @code{FALSE} are recognized.
Parentheses group sub-expressions.

@node FORTRAN Object Orientation
@section Object Orientation

@cindex OOPS
@cindex NEW
@cindex INVOKE
@cindex SUPER
@cindex CLASS

The FORTRAN dialect includes object-oriented extensions that compile to
OOPS calls:

@table @asis
@item @code{CLASS @var{name} @dots{} END @var{name}}
Class body; compiles to a @code{:fortran-class} node.
@item @code{CLASS #:@var{method} @dots{} ENDMETHOD}
Class method definition.
@item @code{INSTANCE @var{variable} = NEW @var{class-name}(@var{args})}
Instantiation; compiles to a @code{:fortran-new} node.
@item @code{INVOKE @var{object}.@var{method}(@var{args})}
Message send; compiles to a @code{:invoke} node.
@item @code{INVOKE SUPER.@var{method}(@var{args})}
Superclass dispatch; compiles to a @code{:sup-call} node.
@end table

@node FORTRAN Limitations
@section Designed-Grammar Caveats

@cindex lexer limitations

@itemize
@item The front end is disabled in the build (@code{#+ ()} in
@file{eightbol.asd}); it is not reachable from the command line.
@item The lexer drops the last character of an identifier, and cannot
tokenize a single-character identifier at all.  Until this is fixed,
identifiers longer than one character lose their final character.
@item The lexer emits keyword symbols that never match the parser's
terminals, so even correct text can fail to parse.
@item Several grammar non-terminals referenced by the rules (for
example the @code{arg-list?} forms and the loop terminator variants) are
still being reconciled; the parser compiles, but untested end to end.
@end itemize

Until these are addressed, treat the FORTRAN front end as documentation
of intent rather than as a working compiler.

@node Lingo
@chapter Lingo

@cindex Lingo
@cindex Director
@cindex designed grammar

Lingo is the scripting language of Macromedia Director.  EIGHTBOL's
Lingo front end targets the classic Director MX-era dialect, adapted to
8-bit constraints.  Like the FORTRAN front end, it is a @strong{designed
grammar}: it is disabled in the build, not reachable from the command
line, and its lexer has structural defects (see @ref{Lingo
Limitations}).

@menu
* Lingo Program Structure::     Modules and handlers
* Lingo Statements::            Statement reference
* Lingo Limitations::           Designed grammar caveats
@end menu

@node Lingo Program Structure
@section Program Structure

A Lingo program is a sequence of module definitions.  Each module is
either a handler (function-like) or a procedure:

@example
on tick
  set Ticks to Ticks + 1
  return Ticks
end

on init
  Ticks = 0
end
@end example

Lingo's @code{set @var{x} to @var{y}} and assignment (@code{@var{x} =
@var{y}}) forms are both accepted; assignments compile to
@code{:move} nodes.

@node Lingo Statements
@section Statements

@table @asis
@item @code{set @var{target} to @var{expression}}
@itemx @code{@var{target} = @var{expression}}
Assignment; compiles to a @code{:move}.
@item @code{move @var{expression} to @var{target}}
Copy a value.
@item @code{property @var{list}}
Property declaration; compiles to a @code{:move} of the property list.
@item @code{call @var{target}(@var{args})}
Function call.
@item @code{call @var{target}(@var{args} @dots{} "library" @dots{})}
Library call; compiled with the library flag set.
@item @code{for @var{var} in @var{start} to @var{end} step @var{step} do @dots{} end}
Counted loop; compiles to a @code{:perform} node.
@item @code{while @var{condition} do @dots{} end}
Conditional loop.
@item @code{if @var{condition} then @dots{} else @dots{} end if}
Conditional execution.
@item @code{return}
Return; compiles to a @code{:goback}.
@item @code{return @var{expression}}
Return a value; compiles to a @code{:move} into @code{RESULT}.
@item @code{goto @var{label}}
Unconditional jump.
@item @code{@var{object} : @var{method}(@var{args})}
@itemx @code{@var{object}.@var{method}(@var{args})}
Message send; compiles to an @code{:invoke}.
@end table

@node Lingo Limitations
@section Designed-Grammar Caveats

@itemize
@item The front end is disabled in the build; it is not reachable from
the command line.
@item The lexer contains an unbalanced parenthesis that closes the
label/name rule early and orphans a loop that calls undefined functions;
it cannot tokenize real Lingo text.
@item The token scheme the parser expects can never match what the lexer
produces, so no input can parse end to end.
@item Sprites, scores, and Director's media model are outside the scope
of the EIGHTBOL dialect.
@end itemize

@node Lua
@chapter Lua

@cindex Lua
@cindex designed grammar

The Lua front end targets a subset of the Lua 5.x language.  It is a
@strong{design sketch}: the parser file is not a real CL-YACC grammar, it
cannot be loaded, and the front end is disabled in the build.  The
syntax below is the syntax the front end intends to accept.

@menu
* Lua Program Structure::       Chunks, data, and statements
* Lua Statements::              Statement reference
* Lua Limitations::             Design-sketch caveats
@end menu

@node Lua Program Structure
@section Program Structure

A Lua program is a @emph{chunk}: an optional set of data definitions
followed by statements.  Data definitions use @code{local}:

@example
local Health = 100
local Table = @{1, 2, 3@}

while Health > 0 do
  Health = Health - 1
end
@end example

Data definitions compile to @code{:dd} nodes; array definitions using
table literals are treated as fixed-size arrays.

@node Lua Statements
@section Statements

@table @asis
@item @code{local @var{var} = @var{value}}
Data definition; compiles to a @code{:dd} node.
@item @code{local @var{arr} = @{@var{e1}, @var{e2}, @dots{}@}}
Array definition; compiles to a @code{:dd} node with an @code{OCCURS}
count equal to the number of elements.
@item @code{@var{target} = @var{expression}}
Assignment; compiles to a @code{:set}.
@item @code{if @var{condition} then @dots{} else @dots{} end}
Conditional execution; compiles to an @code{:if}.
@item @code{while @var{condition} do @dots{} end}
Loop; compiles to a @code{:perform}.
@item @code{for @var{var} = @var{start}, @var{end} do @dots{} end}
Counted loop; compiles to a @code{:perform}.
@item @code{@var{name}(@var{args})}
Function call; compiles to a @code{:call}.
@item @code{@var{object}:@var{method}(@var{args})}
Method call; compiles to a @code{:call} on the object.
@item @code{return @var{expression}}
Return; compiles to an @code{:exit-method}.
@end table

@node Lua Limitations
@section Design-Sketch Caveats

@itemize
@item The front end is disabled in the build; it is not reachable from
the command line.
@item The parser file cannot be loaded at all: it is not a valid CL-YACC
grammar and references unbound symbols.
@item The lexer cannot tokenize decimal literals (the number scanner
fails on a decimal point), and consequently cannot tokenize most real
Lua programs.
@item The language's metatables, closures, and multiple return values are
outside the scope of the EIGHTBOL dialect.
@end itemize

@node Objective-C
@chapter Objective-C

@cindex Objective-C
@cindex designed grammar

The Objective-C front end targets a small object-oriented dialect with
message-passing syntax.  Like FORTRAN, Lingo, and Lua, it is a
@strong{designed grammar}: it is disabled in the build and its parser has
known structural defects (see @ref{Objective-C Limitations}).

@menu
* Objective-C Program Structure::  Interfaces, implementations, and messages
* Objective-C Statements::      Statement reference
* Objective-C Expressions::     Messages, properties, and operators
* Objective-C Exception Handling::  @code{@@try} and friends
* Objective-C Limitations::     Designed grammar caveats
@end menu

@node Objective-C Program Structure
@section Program Structure

A program is a sequence of class definitions, method definitions, and
statements.  Classes use the conventional
@code{@@interface}@dots{}@code{@@end} form:

@example
@@interface Hero : Actor
  int hitPoints;
- (void) heal;
@@end

@@implementation Hero
- (void) heal
@{
  hitPoints = hitPoints + 10;
@}
@@end
@end example

@code{@@interface} and @code{@@implementation} are both recognized;
instance and class method definitions are introduced with @code{-} and
@code{+} respectively.

@node Objective-C Statements
@section Statements

@table @asis
@item @code{@var{target} = @var{expression}}
Assignment; compiles to a @code{:move}.  Assignment through a property
compiles to a move into the @code{@var{Property}Of@var{Object}} slot.
@item @code{[@var{receiver} @var{selector}]}
Message send; compiles to an @code{:invoke}.
@item @code{[@var{receiver} @var{keyword}: @var{arg} @var{keyword}: @var{arg}]}
Keyword message send; the keyword parts are concatenated into the method
name.
@item @code{if (@var{condition}) @var{statement}}
@itemx @code{if (@var{condition}) @var{statement} else @var{statement}}
Conditional execution; compiles to an @code{:if}.
@item @code{while (@var{condition}) @var{statement}}
Loop; compiles to a @code{:perform} running until the condition is
false.
@item @code{for (@var{var} = @var{start}; @var{condition}; @var{step}) @var{statement}}
Counted loop; compiles to a @code{:perform}.
@item @code{return @var{expression}}
Return a value.
@item @code{return;}
Return; compiles to a @code{:goback}.
@end table

@node Objective-C Expressions
@section Expressions

Message sends and property access are the distinctive expressions of the
dialect:

@table @asis
@item @code{@var{object}.@var{property}}
Property get; compiles to a message send to the getter.
@item @code{self}, @code{super}
The current object and its superclass; @code{super} is accepted as a
receiver.
@item @code{nil}, @code{YES}, @code{NO}, @code{true}, @code{false}
Literal constants.
@item @code{@@selector(@var{name})}
Selectors are recognized by the token list.
@end table

Arithmetic and relational operators are @code{+} @code{-} @code{*}
@code{/}, @code{==} @code{!=} @code{<} @code{<=} @code{>} @code{>=},
plus the logical @code{&&} @code{||} @code{!}.

@node Objective-C Exception Handling
@section Exception Handling

@cindex @code{@@try}
@cindex @code{@@throw}

Exception directives are recognized by the lexer but the designed
behavior is to lower them to EIGHTBOL fault handling:

@table @asis
@item @code{@@try} @dots{} @code{@@catch} @dots{} @code{@@finally}
Lowered to @code{:log-fault} handling.
@item @code{@@throw}
Lowered to a @code{:debug-break}.
@end table

@node Objective-C Limitations
@section Designed-Grammar Caveats

@itemize
@item The front end is disabled in the build; it is not reachable from
the command line.
@item The lexer has the same unbalanced-parenthesis defect as the Lingo
lexer (the label rule closes early), so it cannot tokenize real
Objective-C text.
@item The parser's parentheses are unbalanced and the @code{eval-when}
form is never closed; the parser cannot be compiled.
@item Protocols, categories, properties, and the full runtime model are
outside the scope of the EIGHTBOL dialect.
@end itemize

@node Pascal
@chapter Pascal

@cindex Pascal
@cindex Turbo Pascal

The Pascal front end compiles a dialect derived from Turbo Pascal 7.0,
with adjustments for EIGHTBOL's storage model.  It is the most complete
of the non-COBOL, non-BASIC front ends and is enabled in the build
(loaded from @file{eightbol.asd}); however it is not yet wired into the
command line.

@menu
* Pascal Program Structure::    Methods, procedures, and bodies
* Pascal Statements::           Statement reference
* Pascal Operators and Expressions::  Operator reference
* Pascal Unsupported Constructs::  What EIGHTBOL Pascal does not do
@end menu

@node Pascal Program Structure
@section Program Structure

A Pascal program is a list of methods.  Each method is a
@code{PROCEDURE} whose body is a @code{BEGIN}@dots{}@code{END} block.
A procedure may be attached to a class with @code{ON}:

@example
PROCEDURE Bump;
BEGIN
  Ticks := Ticks + 1;
END;

PROCEDURE Heal ON Hero;
BEGIN
  HP := HP + 10;
END;
@end example

Procedures attached to a class compile to a method named
@code{@var{Class}#@var{Procedure}}.

@node Pascal Statements
@section Statements

@table @asis
@item @code{@var{target} := @var{expression}}
Assignment; compiles to a @code{:move}.  (@code{=} is accepted as an
alias in some contexts.)
@item @code{MOVE @var{expression} @var{target}}
Copy a value.
@item @code{CALL @var{name}}
Call a named target.
@item @code{CALL @var{method} ON @var{object}}
Method invocation; compiles to an @code{:invoke}.
@item @code{CALL @var{name} IN @var{bank}}
Call a routine in a named bank.
@item @code{CALL @var{name} IN LIBRARY}
Resident-library call.
@item @code{IF @var{condition} THEN @var{statements}}
@itemx @code{IF @var{condition} THEN @var{statements} ELSE @var{statements}}
Conditional execution; compiles to an @code{:if}.
@item @code{WHILE @var{condition} NEXT @var{statements}}
Loop; compiles to a @code{:perform}.
@item @code{UNTIL @var{condition} NEXT @var{statements}}
Loop.
@item @code{DO NEXT WHILE @var{condition} @var{statements}}
@itemx @code{DO NEXT UNTIL @var{condition} @var{statements}}
Do-while variants.
@item @code{FOR @var{var} FROM @var{from} TO @var{to} STEP @var{step} NEXT @var{statements}}
Counted loop; compiles to a @code{:perform}.
@item @code{EXIT}
Exit the current method.
@item @code{GOBACK}
Return from the current method.
@item @code{LOG FAULT @var{expression}}
Signal a fault.
@item @code{STOP @var{expression}}
Debug break with a code.
@item @code{COPY "@var{name}"}
Expand a copybook.
@item @code{@var{number}:} / @code{@var{label}:}
Assembly entry label; compiles to an @code{:assembly-entry}.
@end table

@node Pascal Operators and Expressions
@section Operators and Expressions

Expressions are built from identifiers, numbers, and strings, combined
with the binary operators @code{+} @code{-} @code{*} @code{/},
relational @code{=} @code{<} @code{>} @code{<=} @code{>=} @code{<>},
and logical @code{AND} @code{OR} @code{NOT}.  A unary minus is
recognized.  Precedence follows Pascal: multiplication and division bind
more tightly than addition, relational operators bind more loosely, and
logical @code{AND}/@code{OR} lowest.

@node Pascal Unsupported Constructs
@section Constructs EIGHTBOL Pascal Does Not Support

@itemize
@item @code{WITH} --- not supported; use fully qualified names.
@item Records and variants --- records map to @code{:dd} storage nodes;
record field access syntax is not supported.
@item File I/O --- @code{WRITE}, @code{WRITELN}, @code{READ},
@code{READLN}, and file variables are not supported.
@item @code{CASE} --- not in the grammar.
@item Arrays, pointers, and @code{NEW}/@code{DISPOSE} --- not supported.
@item The loop grammar drops the loop body in current code (a known
limitation of the @code{:perform} construction).
@end itemize

@node SmallTalk
@chapter SmallTalk

@cindex SmallTalk
@cindex designed grammar

The SmallTalk front end targets a minimal SmallTalk dialect: assignment,
message sending, literals, and variables.  It is enabled in the build but
the implementation is incomplete --- the lexer is a copy of the defective
Lingo lexer, the parser has no keywords beyond the bare minimum, and no
SmallTalk symbols are exported from the package (see @ref{SmallTalk
Limitations}).

@menu
* SmallTalk Program Structure::  Statements and messages
* SmallTalk Statements::        Statement reference
* SmallTalk Limitations::       Implementation caveats
@end menu

@node SmallTalk Program Structure
@section Program Structure

A SmallTalk program is a list of statements: assignments, message sends,
and expressions.  Statements are evaluated in order:

@example
Ticks := 0.
self update.
Ticks := Ticks + 1.
@end example

Statements are separated by periods, in the SmallTalk manner.  Message
sends compile to @code{:invoke} nodes.

@node SmallTalk Statements
@section Statements

@table @asis
@item @code{@var{variable} := @var{expression}}
Assignment; compiles to a @code{:move}.
@item @code{@var{receiver} @var{selector} @dots{}}
Message send; compiles to an @code{:invoke} with the selector as the
method name and the arguments (if any) passed through.
@item @code{@var{expression}}
An expression statement; typically a literal or variable.
@item @code{@var{number}} / @code{'@var{string}'}
Literals.
@end table

@node SmallTalk Limitations
@section Implementation Caveats

@itemize
@item The lexer is a byte-identical copy of the defective Lingo lexer,
so it cannot tokenize real SmallTalk text.
@item The parser grammar contains only assignments, messages, and
literals; there are no blocks, temporaries, or control structures.
@item No SmallTalk symbols are exported from the package, so nothing
invokes the front end.
@item @file{smalltalk-parser.lisp} is a symbolic link to a file outside
the repository; a real, committed parser must replace it.
@end itemize

@node Abstract Syntax Tree
@chapter Abstract Syntax Tree

@cindex abstract syntax tree
@cindex AST
@cindex plist

Every front end compiles its source into the same internal
representation: an abstract syntax tree (AST).  The AST is a tree of
plain Common Lisp plists, so it can be printed, read, edited, and
serialized without any special format.  The back ends consume the AST
and produce assembly.

This chapter is the reference for the AST syntax.  It is most useful to
two audiences: people debugging a compiler pass, and people who want to
inspect the AST EIGHTBOL generates for a source file.

@menu
* AST Program and Method::      Program, method, and procedure nodes
* AST Statements::              Executable statement nodes
* AST Operands::                Operands, identifiers, and literals
* AST Serialization::           Reading and writing AST files
@end menu

@node AST Program and Method
@section Program and Method Nodes

@table @asis
@item @code{(:program :class-id "@var{name}" :data (...) :methods (...) :identification ... :environment ...)}
The root of every compiled object.  @code{:data} holds the data items
(@code{:dd} nodes) and @code{:methods} the method and procedure nodes.
@item @code{(:method :method-id "@var{name}" :statements (...))}
An object method.  The first statement may be an
@code{:assembly-entry} node naming the assembly label.
@item @code{(:procedure :name "@var{name}" :statements (...))}
A standalone routine.
@item @code{(:dd :level @var{n} :label "@var{name}" :attr (...))}
A data definition.  Attributes include @code{:usage}, @code{:value},
and @code{:occurs}.
@end table

@node AST Statements
@section Statement Nodes

@table @asis
@item @code{(:move :from @var{expr} :to @var{identifier})}
Assignment and @code{MOVE}.
@item @code{(:invoke :object @var{expr} :method "@var{name}" [:returning @var{id}])}
Method invocation.
@item @code{(:call-acc :target @var{name} :bank @var{bank-or-nil})}
A CALL that passes a value in the accumulator.  Also
@code{(:call :target @var{name} :bank @var{b} :library t)} for library
calls and @code{(:call :service @var{name})} for service dispatch.
@item @code{(:if :condition @var{cond} :then @var{stmts} :else @var{stmts})}
Conditional execution.
@item @code{(:goto :target @var{identifier})}
Unconditional jump.
@item @code{(:goback)}
Return from the current routine.
@item @code{(:exit-method)}
@itemx @code{(:exit-program)}
@itemx @code{(:exit)}
Exit forms.
@item @code{(:stop-run)}
@itemx @code{(:stop-run :code "@var{code}")}
Stop execution, optionally with a code.
@item @code{(:add :from @var{expr} :to @var{identifier})}
@itemx @code{(:add :from @var{expr} :to @var{expr} :giving @var{identifier})}
Addition.
@item @code{(:subtract :subtrahend @var{expr} :from @var{expr} [:giving @var{id}])}
Subtraction.
@item @code{(:multiply :by @var{expr} :multiplier @var{expr} [:giving @var{id}])}
Multiplication.
@item @code{(:divide :numerator @var{expr} :denominator @var{expr} [:giving @var{id}])}
Division.
@item @code{(:compute :target @var{identifier} :expression @var{expr})}
Computed assignment.
@item @code{(:perform :procedure @var{name} [:times @var{expr}] [:until @var{cond}] [:varying @var{var} :from @var{expr} :by @var{expr}])}
Loop or paragraph call.
@item @code{(:set :target @var{identifier} :value @var{expr})}
Assignment.
@item @code{(:log-fault :code @var{dword-expr})}
Fault signalling.
@item @code{(:debug-break :code @var{expr})}
Debug break.
@item @code{(:copy :name "@var{CopybookName}")}
Residual copybook reference after a failed expansion.
@item @code{(:string-blt :source @var{operand} :dest @var{operand} [:length @var{expr}])}
@code{STRING DELIMITED BY SIZE} block transfer.
@item @code{(:assembly-entry :label "@var{Symbol}")}
Names the assembly entry symbol; must be the first executable-area
statement.
@item @code{(:error :code @var{text})}
An error node produced by the parser's error recovery.
@end table

FORTRAN-specific nodes carry explicit type information:

@table @asis
@item @code{(:fortran-move :from @var{expr} :to @var{id} :target-type @var{type})}
@itemx @code{(:fortran-compute :target @var{id} :expression @var{expr} :result-type @var{type})}
@itemx @code{(:fortran-declare :name @var{id} :type @var{type} [:init @var{expr}])}
@itemx @code{(:fortran-do :var @var{id} :from @var{expr} :to @var{expr} [:by @var{expr}] :stmts @var{stmts})}
@itemx @code{(:fortran-if :condition @var{expr} :then @var{stmts} :else @var{stmts})}
@itemx @code{(:fortran-arithmetic :op @var{kw} :left @var{expr} :right @var{expr} :result-type @var{type})}
@end table

@node AST Operands
@section Operands

Operands and expressions appearing in the nodes above take these forms:

@table @asis
@item number
A numeric literal.
@item string
A string literal.
@item symbol
An identifier reference.
@item @code{(:of @var{slot} @var{obj})}
Qualified identifier (@code{slot OF obj}).
@item @code{(:address-of @var{id})}
@code{ADDRESS OF id}.
@item @code{(:refmod :base @var{name} :start @var{expr} :length @var{expr})}
Reference modification, @code{name(start:length)}.
@item @code{(:subscript @var{name} @var{index})}
Subscripted identifier.
@item @code{:self} / @code{:null}
@code{SELF} and @code{NULL}.
@item @code{(= @var{a} @var{b})}, @code{(/= @var{a} @var{b})},
@code{(< @var{a} @var{b})}, @code{(<= @var{a} @var{b})},
@code{(> @var{a} @var{b})}, @code{(>= @var{a} @var{b})}
Relational conditions.
@item @code{(:and @var{a} @var{b})}, @code{(:or @var{a} @var{b})},
@code{(:not @var{a})}
Logical conditions.
@item @code{(:bit-and @var{a} @var{b})}, @code{(:bit-or @var{a} @var{b})},
@code{(:bit-xor @var{a} @var{b})}, @code{(:bit-not @var{a})},
@code{(:shift-left @var{a} @var{n})}, @code{(:shift-right @var{a} @var{n})}
Bit operations.
@item @code{(:null @var{expr})} / @code{(:not-null @var{expr})}
Null tests.
@end table

@node AST Serialization
@section Reading and Writing AST Files

Because the AST is plain plists, it round-trips through
@code{print}@dots{}@code{read}.  An AST may be written to a file, edited
by hand, and passed back to the compiler with
@code{compile-eightbol-from-ast}, which accepts an AST (or a path to a
file holding one) and a list of target CPUs:

@example
(compile-eightbol-from-ast ast :cpus '(:6502))
@end example

@node ABI
@chapter ABI Formats and Assembler Syntax

@cindex ABI
@cindex assembler syntax
@cindex register usage

Every back end follows the same calling convention: a single value is
passed in the machine's first register and returned in the same
register; everything else travels through global variables.  See
@ref{Data and Parameters}.  This chapter records the assembler dialect,
register conventions, and invocation text for each supported processor.

@menu
* Common ABI::                  Shared conventions and naming
* 6502 Family ABI::             6502, 65c02, 65c816, HuC6280, RP2A03
* Z80 ABI::                     Zilog Z80
* SM83 ABI::                    Sharp SM83 (Game Boy)
* cp1610 ABI::                  Intellivision
* m6800 ABI::                   Motorola 6800
* m68k ABI::                    Motorola 68000
* i286 ABI::                    Intel 80286
* ARM7 ABI::                    ARM7TDMI (Game Boy Advance)
* F8 ABI::                      Fairchild F8 (Channel F)
@end menu

@node Common ABI
@section Common Conventions

@cindex method labels
@cindex OOPS
@cindex variable labels

@itemize
@item Method entry labels follow the OOPS convention
@code{Method@var{ClassName}@var{MethodName}} on the 6502 family.
Hyphenated names translate to underscores (@code{Song--Heal--ID} emits
@code{Song_Heal_ID}).
@item Global variables are referred to by their derived assembly
symbols; class slots are fetched through the object pointer.
@item A method returns with the platform's return instruction
(@code{rts} on the 6502 family and Z80, @code{ret} on the i286 and
SM83, @code{pul pc} on the cp1610, @code{rts} on the m68k, and so on).
@end itemize

@node 6502 Family ABI
@section 6502, 65c02, 65c816, HuC6280, RP2A03

@cindex 64tass
@cindex 6502 registers

Output uses 64tass assembler syntax.  Register usage: @code{A} is the
accumulator (the calling-convention register), @code{X} carries
subscript indices or temporary values, and @code{Y} carries slot
offsets.  Array fetches use @code{X}; slot-of-self fetches use
@code{Y}.

@table @asis
@item @code{INVOKE Var "M"}
Emits @code{.CallMethod  Call@var{Type}M, @var{Type}Class} (a Phantasia
OOPS macro expanding to @code{jsr DoCallMethod}), not a bare
@code{jsr Invoke@dots{}} label.
@item @code{CALL @var{name} IN LIBRARY}
Emits @code{jsr Lib.@var{name}} for a resident-library call.
@item @code{CALL SERVICE @var{name}}
Emits a service-dispatch call whose bank is resolved at link time
(@code{.FarCall} / @code{.FarJSR}).
@item @code{LOG FAULT}
Emits @code{.LogFault "@var{code}"}, a 4-character fault string.
@item @code{BREAK}
Emits @code{brk}.
@end table

CPU-specific instruction sets (64tass format):

@table @asis
@item @code{:6502}
Plain 6502: @code{jmp} for unconditional branches; @code{SED}/@code{CLD}
for BCD arithmetic; undocumented opcodes (@code{lax}, @code{dcp}) where
helpful.
@item @code{:65c02}
Adds @code{bra}, @code{stz}, @code{trb}, @code{tsb}; no undocumented
opcodes.
@item @code{:65c816}
Like the 65c02 in emulation mode; 64tass syntax.
@item @code{:huc6280}
Like the 65c02; bank switching via macros.  Executes all 6502
instructions; delegates code generation to the 6502 backend.
@item @code{:rp2a03}
NES CPU.  6502 subset with no decimal mode: no @code{SED}; BCD and
packed-decimal arithmetic uses software correction routines instead
(binary ADC, then add 6 to the low nibble if it exceeds 9, and so on).
@end table

@node Z80 ABI
@section Zilog Z80

@cindex Z80 registers

Output is lowercase Z80 assembly in the common dialect (@code{ld a,(hl)},
and so on).  Registers: @code{A} is the accumulator and calling-
convention register; @code{HL} is the pointer/result register;
@code{BC} is the count; @code{DE} is a destination pointer or temp
(preferred over the stack).  Control flow uses @code{jp}, @code{jr},
@code{call}, @code{ret}, and @code{djnz}.

@node SM83 ABI
@section Sharp SM83 (Game Boy)

@cindex RGBDS

Output is RGBDS assembler syntax (@code{ld a,[hl]}, @code{jr nz}, and so
on).  The register conventions mirror the Z80: @code{A} accumulator,
@code{HL} pointer/result, @code{BC} count, @code{DE} destination/temp.

@node cp1610 ABI
@section Intellivision cp1610

@cindex as1600

Output is as1600 assembler syntax.  The cp1610 uses the classic
instruction set @code{MVII} (move immediate), @code{MVI} (load),
@code{MVO} (store), @code{ADDR}, and @code{SUBR}.  A single 16-bit
register carries the calling-convention value.

@node m6800 ABI
@section Motorola 6800

@cindex Vectrex

A minimal m6800 emitter intended for Vectrex-style Motorola-family
workflows.  Uses plain 6800 mnemonics (@code{LDAA}, @code{STAA},
@code{CMPA}, @code{BEQ}, @code{BNE}, @code{JSR}, @code{JMP}, @code{RTS})
with direct symbols for generated labels.

@node m68k ABI
@section Motorola 68000

@cindex 68000

Targets the Motorola 68000 instruction set only.  Data registers
@code{D0}--@code{D7}, address registers @code{A0}--@code{A7} (@code{A7}
is the stack pointer).  Instructions: @code{move}, @code{add},
@code{sub}, @code{cmp}, @code{and}, @code{or}, @code{eor}, @code{not},
@code{neg}, @code{clr}; control: @code{jsr}, @code{jmp}, @code{rts},
@code{bra}, and the conditional branches.  Operand sizes are @code{.b}
(byte), @code{.w} (word), and @code{.l} (long).  The calling-convention
value rides in @code{D0}.

@node i286 ABI
@section Intel 80286

@cindex 16-bit
@cindex Intel syntax

A 16-bit backend producing Intel-syntax assembly (@code{mov ax, bx},
@code{mov al, [si]}).  Registers: @code{AX} accumulator and calling-
convention register, @code{BX} temp, @code{SI} index, @code{DI}
destination pointer, @code{CX} count; segments @code{CS}, @code{DS},
@code{SS}, @code{ES}.  Control: @code{call}, @code{ret}, @code{jmp},
@code{jz}, @code{jnz}.

@node ARM7 ABI
@section ARM7TDMI (Game Boy Advance)

@cindex Thumb
@cindex GAS

Targets the Game Boy Advance ARM7TDMI (@code{armv4t}).  Thumb is the
default code path for size, using unified GAS syntax with
@code{.thumb}, @code{.syntax unified}, and @code{.cpu arm7tdmi}.
Instructions used: @code{movs}, @code{ldr}/@code{ldrb}/@code{ldrh},
@code{str}/@code{strb}/@code{strh}, @code{add}, @code{subs}, @code{cmp},
@code{b}/@code{bl}/@code{bne}@dots{}, @code{bx lr}.  Register usage:
@code{r0} is the value and calling-convention register; @code{r1},
@code{r2}, @code{r3} are scratch and address registers.

@node F8 ABI
@section Fairchild F8 (Channel F)

@cindex F8
@cindex Channel F

Emits Fairchild F8 assembly with the Turner/Fairchild mnemonics: the
@code{A} accumulator, @code{DC0} data counter, @code{LM}/@code{ST},
@code{DCI}, @code{LI}/@code{AI}/@code{CI}, @code{LR H/DC0}, branches
@code{BR}/@code{BZ}/@code{BNZ}@dots{}, and subroutine call/return
@code{PI}/@code{POP}.  16-bit values use scratch 10 (lo) and 11 (hi);
scratch 6--9 are code-generation temporaries.

@node Error Conditions
@appendix Error Conditions and Fault Codes

@cindex errors
@cindex faults
@cindex conditions

This appendix lists the compiler's error conditions and the runtime
fault mechanism.

@menu
* Compiler Errors::             Compile-time condition hierarchy
* Runtime Faults::              The fault system and fault codes
@end menu

@node Compiler Errors
@section Compiler Errors

All compiler errors are subtypes of @code{compiler-error} and carry a
@code{message}.  The hierarchy, from most to least specific:

@table @asis
@item @code{source-error}
A source-level error: a parser or unsupported-statement failure.  Reports
the file, line, and sequence number when known.
@item @code{lexer-error}
A lexing failure; reports the file and line plus the offending form.
@item @code{copybook-error}
A copybook failure.  Subtypes: @code{copybook-not-found},
@code{copybook-invalid-name}, @code{copybook-read-error}.
@item @code{backend-error}
A code-generation failure for a specific CPU.  Subtypes:
@code{backend-ast-error} (unexpected AST shape),
@code{backend-condition-not-implemented},
@code{backend-string-blt-error},
@code{backend-call-service-error}, @code{backend-copy-not-expanded}.
@item @code{compile-error}
A pipeline failure; reports the compilation stage and input.  Subtypes:
@code{input-file-not-found}, @code{parse-failed-error},
@code{invalid-ast-error}, @code{undefined-class-reference},
@code{routine-not-terminated}.
@item @code{usage-error}
A command-line error.  Subtypes: @code{unknown-option-error},
@code{dangling-option-error}, @code{unknown-cpu-error}.
@end table

@node Runtime Faults
@section Runtime Faults

@cindex LOG FAULT
@cindex LogFault

At runtime, @code{LOG FAULT} (or @code{LogFault}) signals a fault
through the platform fault handler.  Fault codes are four-character
strings; the assembler renders them in the platform's minifont for the
debugger.  A @code{STOP RUN} halts the program.  On the 6502 family a
@code{BREAK} statement fires the processor's @code{brk} to enter the
monitor.

